    summary: "A rotating gantry is the largest and most expensive part of a proton centre. Upright systems remove it: the beam stays horizontal and a robotic chair rotates and tilts the patient in front of it, with an upright CT scanner for imaging in the treatment position. P-Cure's proton system was cleared by the FDA on 20 March 2023 (K221996) as a synchrotron with a single fixed beam and a seated patient, for the head, neck and thorax; its 2025 clearance (K242418) allows seated or supine treatment. Leo Cancer Care's Marie positioning and imaging system was cleared on 25 July 2025 (K250970) for treatment in the \"upright position rather than conventional supine treatments\", with its Eve patient positioner cleared in 2024 (K231612), and was CE-marked in May 2026. Mevion combined Marie with its compact cyclotron in the S250-FIT, cleared on 12 September 2025 (K250986); the 510(k) summary limits fully seated treatment to \"sites above the mid-chest or carina\". Mevion states that the system fits \"a standard 110 m² (1,200 sq ft) LINAC vault\", and the first patient was treated at Stanford in June 2026.\n\nUpright treatment is old in fixed-beam particle rooms and in some photon work, but it needs imaging in the upright position because organs sit differently when a person sits. The open questions are which sites it suits beyond the head, neck and upper chest, how reproducible daily set-up is, and whether patients who struggle to lie flat (with breathing difficulty, pain or swallowing problems) find it easier; no randomised comparison with supine treatment was found.",
