    summary: "In the United States a treatment chair is a Class I device exempt from premarket notification if unpowered (21 CFR 880.6140, \"intended for use of blood donors, geriatric patients, or patients undergoing treatment or examination\", product code FRK) and a Class II device exempt on conditions if electrically positioned (21 CFR 890.3110, product code INO), the conditions being tests that safety controls prevent falls, that the chair bears its rated weight and that it lasts its service life. No product code names infusion or oncology. The mapping of powered infusion recliners to INO and unpowered ones to FRK is OnCo's reading of the definitions, not an FDA statement.\n\nMakers sell recliners for infusion bays: Champion Healthcare Solutions (formerly Champion Manufacturing, renamed in January 2025, which acquired Winco in 2021) markets the Alō and Ascent lines as \"Oncology & Infusion Medical Recliners\", describing \"powered recline and supine Trendelenburg positioning, a quick-release seat for complete cleaning and disinfecting, swing-away arms\"; Kwalu describes its Ellie recliner for \"infusion bays\". OnCo found no published study of chair comfort, pressure injury or ergonomics in oncology patients. The nearest is a pressure-mapping study of a hospital recliner in 34 healthy adults, in which recline position \"had no association with interface pressure outcomes\" while body weight and seat surface did. Chair time is itself a constraint: a 2025 review of scalp cooling notes that \"chair time, and equipment limitations all impact\" its use and that cooling stations outside the infusion chair have been proposed, and trials and registry data show that shorter post-infusion cooling keeps most of the benefit.",
    principle: "A reclining chair positions the patient for venous access and long infusions, with powered recline that can tilt head-down (Trendelenburg), armrests for the infusion arm and surfaces that can be cleaned between patients.",
