    summary: "Before machine scalp coolers were cleared, hair preservation meant a series of frozen gel caps, swapped as each warmed. They are still used, rented by the month and carried in by patients, because they need no machine in the unit. Every FDA authorisation under the scalp-cooling classification (21 CFR 878.4360, product code PMC) belongs to a refrigerated machine; no manual cap appears there. Penguin's own page says only that the materials are approved in Canada and by the FDA, and a gel cap listed by Polygel LLC as \"Chemo Cold Cap\" is registered as a reusable hot or cold pack (Class I, exempt), not cleared for alopecia.\n\nPublished results are single-institution series. In a retrospective study of 100 patients using Penguin caps, 92.1 per cent of 89 evaluable kept at least half their hair, with 71.4 per cent success on doxorubicin-containing regimens against 95.7 per cent with paclitaxel and 96.6 per cent with docetaxel. Without a randomised comparison against machines, the trade-off is practical: caps are portable and need no unit equipment, but the temperature varies as each cap warms and a helper must change them on time.",
